  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Why do we need to implement a fault-tolerant system? State some of its limitations. (3 marks)', 'A fault-tolerant system continues to operate correctly even when some of its components fail, using redundancy such as RAID disks, backup servers, dual power supplies and failover clustering. It is needed for critical services (banking, hospitals, e-government, data centres) to ensure high availability, prevent data loss and downtime, and keep users'' trust. Limitations: it is costly because hardware, software and power are duplicated; design, testing and maintenance are more complex; it needs more space and energy; replication and checking can reduce performance; and it cannot protect against every fault - for example a design bug or a disaster affecting all copies at once.', null, '[{"point": "Need: keeps the system working when a component fails - high availability, no data loss, continuity of critical services (banking, hospitals, government), reliability", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Limitations: higher cost (redundant hardware, software, power), more complexity to design and maintain, extra space/ energy, may not cover every failure (common-mode failures), reduced performance from replication", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
